<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span>2 </span>Depth Association and 3D Backprojection</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{u}_{i},\mathbf{f}_{i})\in\mathcal{F}_{t}^{\mathrm{static}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>static</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{u}_{i},\mathbf{f}_{i})\in\mathcal{F}_{t}^{\mathrm{static}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the depth value <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}=D_{t}(\mathbf{u}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}=D_{t}(\mathbf{u}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is retrieved from the predicted depth map. To reject unreliable predictions, depths are clipped to the interval <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[d_{\min},d_{\max}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[d_{\min},d_{\max}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Each valid keypoint is then backprojected into the 3D camera frame using the pinhole model:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{i}=d_{i}K^{-1}[\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\top}\ 1]^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{i}=d_{i}K^{-1}[\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\top}\ 1]^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The front end thus produces a set of metrically scaled 3D point–descriptor pairs:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{t}=\{(\mathbf{P}_{i},\mathbf{f}_{i})\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{t}=\{(\mathbf{P}_{i},\mathbf{f}_{i})\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which serves as input to the SLAM back end. This sparse representation retains compatibility with geometric SLAM while embedding absolute scale from the depth network.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Back End Tracking and Mapping</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is passed to the RGB-D interface of an unmodified SLAM system. We adopt ORB-SLAM3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, which performs tracking, mapping, and loop closure within a standard geometric optimization framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Camera tracking is formulated as a Perspective-n-Point (PnP) problem within a RANSAC loop, aligning the 3D points in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with their 2D projections to estimate the current pose. Mapping proceeds by instantiating new 3D map points when parallax and visibility conditions are met. Loop closures are identified via bag-of-words retrieval over ORB descriptors and verified geometrically; verified matches trigger global pose graph optimization and bundle adjustment to refine the trajectory and structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A key design choice is the treatment of predicted depth during optimization. Depth values are treated as fixed priors rather than as variables to be optimized. Each depth introduces a unary residual with variance <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{d}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{d}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in bundle adjustment, encoding the assumed confidence of the depth estimator. This formulation stabilizes global scale while allowing the optimizer to refine structure via multi-view constraints—without requiring any modification to the back end.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our system on the TUM RGB-D benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, using both static and dynamic indoor sequences. Experiments assess overall SLAM accuracy and isolate the contributions of monocular depth estimation, dynamic object filtering, and learned keypoint detection. Unless otherwise noted, results are reported as the root-mean-square error (RMSE) of Absolute Trajectory Error (ATE) in meters, which quantifies the global alignment between estimated and ground-truth camera trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are conducted on an Intel i9-13900K CPU with an NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU. Vision modules execute in parallel on CUDA streams, while the SLAM back end runs on the CPU.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic scenes.</span> We use the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">freiburg3</span> sequences <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">walking_xyz</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">walking_rpy</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">walking_halfsphere</span>, which contain significant non-rigid human motion. High-precision motion-capture ground truth makes these sequences well-suited for benchmarking robustness under dynamic conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Static scenes.</span> To assess geometric consistency and long-term scale stability, we use nine rigid indoor sequences from the <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">freiburg1</span> subset (<span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">360</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">desk</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">desk2</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">floor</span>, etc.) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, which capture standard conditions without dynamic interference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Baselines</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare against both classical and modern SLAM pipelines. RGB-D methods include ORB-SLAM2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, DynaSLAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, DG-SLAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, and RoDynSLAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. Monocular baselines span sparse and dense formulations, including DSO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, DROID-SLAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and recent approaches incorporating dynamic modeling or Gaussian splatting such as WildGS-SLAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and DynaMon <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Performance in Static Environments</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ III-A1 Dynamic Feature Filtering ‣ III-A Front-End Processing Pipeline ‣ III Method ‣ Dropping the D: RGB-D SLAM Without the Depth Sensor" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table I</span></a> reports results on the static subset. Our approach achieves a mean ATE of 7.4 cm with UniDepthV2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and 8.3 cm with DepthAnythingV2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. While competitive, these results are surpassed by fully learned, end-to-end pipelines such as MASt3R-SLAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> (3.0 cm) and DROID-SLAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> (3.8 cm), which jointly optimize depth, pose, and optical flow in a dense bundle adjustment framework. Such coupling enables correction of systematic errors in initial depth predictions, achieving higher global consistency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, even the strongest monocular methods outperform our RGB-D baseline (6.8 cm ATE). This discrepancy stems from the structured-light depth sensor used in TUM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, which produces sparse and noisy depth on reflective or textureless surfaces. Learned monocular models, by contrast, infer dense, coherent geometry in these regions, effectively inpainting missing structure and yielding more stable trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike end-to-end pipelines, our modular system does not refine depth estimates during optimization. While this limits correction of systematic errors, it highlights the strength of pretrained monocular depth networks, which already provide cues sufficient for competitive performance in a classical feature-based SLAM framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Performance in Dynamic Environments</span></h3>

<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
